# 01 — THE NON-NEGOTIABLE QUALITY CONTRACT

The following requirements take precedence over convenience, implementation shortcuts, and superficial completion.

## 1.1 Build a real 3D experience

The three-dimensional environment must occupy a meaningful, central role in the user journey.

The visitor must be able to perceive:

* Physical scale.
* Depth.
* Perspective.
* Spatial relationships.
* Camera movement.
* Material differences.
* Environmental lighting.
* Genuine object interaction.
* A sense of travelling between distinct places.

A background animation behind ordinary website sections does not satisfy this requirement.

## 1.2 Build real functionality

Every prominent control must perform its advertised action.

Every calculator must produce correct results.

Every download must generate a genuine downloadable file.

Every editable canvas must permit actual editing.

Every enquiry must follow a real submission and persistence process.

Every protected administration route must enforce authentication and authorisation on the server.

Never use a visual placeholder to represent a completed feature.

## 1.3 Enforce distinct environments

Each major destination must have a recognisable, independently art-directed identity.

Reusing rendering infrastructure is good engineering. Reusing the same scene design, camera animation, lighting arrangement, tunnel, and floating-object composition for every destination is not acceptable.

A destination is not complete simply because its heading and content have changed.

## 1.4 Prioritise quality over feature count

When time or execution resources become constrained, reduce secondary scope before reducing the quality of the essential experience.

Deliver a complete, polished journey before attempting to create numerous unfinished environments.

Do not create ten mediocre scenes when three excellent, fully functional scenes would provide a stronger foundation for the complete system.

## 1.5 Never misrepresent progress

Distinguish clearly between work that is:

* Designed.
* Implemented.
* Tested.
* Verified.
* Blocked.
* Not yet implemented.

A successful build does not prove that an interaction works.

A rendered scene does not prove that navigation works.

A database record does not prove that an email was delivered.

A screenshot does not prove that an editor can export an image.

All completion claims must be supported by appropriate evidence.

