# 12 — PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING

The experience must be designed for real devices, not only for a powerful development machine.

## 12.1 Rendering

Aim for approximately 60 FPS on capable desktop hardware and stable performance around 30 FPS on supported lower-powered devices, where practical.

Measure rather than assume.

Use:

* Instancing.
* Shared geometry.
* Shared materials.
* Optimised textures.
* Controlled shadow maps.
* Selective post-processing.
* Adaptive pixel ratio.
* Reduced mobile scene complexity.
* Lazy-loaded destinations.
* Asset preloading.
* Proper disposal of unused GPU resources.

Avoid excessive transparency, expensive full-screen effects, and large numbers of independently animated objects.

## 12.2 Loading

Load the minimum required to enter the experience.

Do not require every destination to be downloaded before the opening scene becomes available.

Provide useful loading feedback and handle failed assets.

Do not show fake progress percentages.

## 12.3 Mobile adaptation

Create deliberate mobile compositions.

Reposition or simplify scene elements, reduce rendering cost, improve hit areas, and adapt camera movement.

Test touch selection, scrolling, forms, editing tools, and direct navigation.

Do not claim real-device testing unless an actual device has been used.

## 12.4 Error handling

Provide graceful recovery for:

* WebGL failure.
* Asset errors.
* API errors.
* Database outages.
* Email delivery failures.
* Invalid inputs.
* Expired sessions.
* Export failures.
* Unexpected transitions.

No critical failure should leave the user permanently trapped.

